-- memory given back when the work with it is done (2026-11-18): w-alloc's bytes stay valid for the
-- process's life, so a buffer taken for one read and not given back is held until the process ends.
-- with-bytes n f takes n bytes, runs f on their address, gives them back, and answers f's answer, so
-- a program that reads through a buffer holds the buffer only while it reads
free-at : Word → Proc W Unit
free-at a = cmd {lzero} {W} (w-free a)
